course: dictionary
lesson: 8887
title: Shred: tiny pieces and no pieces at all
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A shred is a small, thin piece torn or cut from something. Shred can be a noun (a shred) or a verb (to shred something). Read the context to find which meaning fits.
explain: «Maddie's ball gets chewed to shreds by the neighbour's Staffie.» The ball is not just a bit damaged. It has been torn into lots of small pieces, so to shreds means into small pieces.
explain: «There was not a shred of evidence.» Here a shred means a very small amount. Swap in "none at all" and check: "There was not none at all of evidence" is clumsy, but the sense works: there was no evidence. A shred of evidence means not even a little.
explain: Real house mice build nests out of shredded paper, cloth and fluff. The clue is "build nests out of", so shredded must mean cut or torn into small thin pieces.
fact: A shred is a small, thin piece torn or cut from something.
fact: To shred means to tear or cut something into small thin pieces.
fact: «Not a shred of evidence» means none at all.
fact: «Shredded cheese» and «shredded cabbage» are cut into thin pieces for cooking.
fact: Offices shred papers with private details so that other people cannot read them.
exercise: «Maddie's ball was chewed to shreds by the neighbour's Staffie.» What does to shreds mean? | Bounced high over the fence | Hidden under the bed | Washed clean and dry | *Torn into lots of small pieces
exercise: Mum says, «Shred the cabbage for the coleslaw.» What does shred mean here? | *Cut it into thin, small pieces | Wash it under the tap | Boil it in a pan | Cook it until it is soft
exercise: «There was not a shred of evidence against him.» What does not a shred mean? | A large pile of evidence | *None at all | Evidence written on paper | Some evidence, but only a little
test: «Mice can build nests out of shredded paper and cloth.» What does shredded mean? | Soaked in water until soft | Painted in bright colours | *Torn or cut into small thin pieces | Folded neatly into squares
test: Why do offices shred papers with private details? | To send them by post | *So other people cannot read them | To use them as a nest for mice | To make the papers bigger
exam: A painting «partly shredded itself» after it sold. Which word means to tear something into small thin pieces? | sell | fold | *shred | paint
